index 0000000..777d32c
--- /dev/null
+++ b/todo/dba_integration.md
@@ -0,0 +1,89 @@
+# devicebasedauth.org integration (S5)
+
+Written to be picked up cold. Status lives in [`STATUS.md`](../STATUS.md) row S5.
+
+## Intent
+
+[devicebasedauth.org](https://devicebasedauth.org) ("DBA") issues revocable, device-bound
+credentials: an ES256 JWS listing what one device may do, for which site, when and where. Its
+master plan §9 makes cronly the first relying party after DBA itself. cronly gains:
+
+1. **Device-bound API keys.** A device enrolled at DBA for audience `cronly.org` calls the cronly
+   API with `Authorization: DBA <credential>` plus a `DPoP: <proof>` header signed by its own key
+   (the private key never leaves the device), or with a ≤ 10-minute DBA access token
+   `Authorization: Bearer <token>` whose JWS header `typ` is `dba-access+jwt`.
+2. **Account linking.** A signed-in cronly user links their DBA account once with an OpenID
+   Connect round trip. Credentials from DBA accounts that are not linked are refused.
+
+Existing Clerk sessions, `crk_` API keys and trigger tokens are unchanged.
+
+## Action mapping
+
+DBA publishes cronly's catalog from cronly's own scope list (DBA
+`src/services/first-party.js`): `cronly:read`, `cronly:run`, `cronly:change`, `cronly:prose`,
+`cronly:secrets:write`, plus `cronly:session`. A credential's allowed `cronly:<scope>` actions
+become the request's scopes and go through the same `API_ROUTES` access checks as API keys.
+
+**`cronly:session` does not grant `SESSION`** (decision recorded here; revisit with "Stage B").
+`SESSION` routes create API keys, start Checkout and link/unlink DBA accounts; letting a device
+credential do that would let one device mint broader, non-device-bound credentials or move the
+link. A DBA credential is therefore always `session: false`, like an API key (it can use MCP).
+
+Grants restricted by `resources` are evaluated without a resource and so do not grant their
+scope (fail closed). Mapping resource patterns such as `job:nightly-*` onto job names is a
+follow-up.
+
+## Design
+
+| Piece | Where | Notes |
+|-------|-------|-------|
+| Verifier | `src/vendor/devicebasedauth/` | Unmodified copy of DBA `packages/verify/src`, pinned by commit and SHA-256 (`src/vendor/devicebasedauth.test.js`). One evaluator implementation; never edit locally. |
+| Request auth | `src/auth/dba.js`, `src/auth/middleware.js` | `createSiteAuth({ audience: 'cronly.org', namespace: 'cronly', scopes: USER_SCOPES, linkedUser, issuer, fetch })`, one per issuer at module scope so JWKS, status-list and DPoP replay caches persist per isolate. The request's `env` reaches `linkedUser` through `AsyncLocalStorage`. DBA is tried before Clerk for `DBA …` and for `Bearer …` whose JWS header says `dba-access+jwt`; any other bearer stays Clerk. |
+| Link index | `DbaLinkDO` (`src/durable-objects/dba-link-do.js`), binding `DBA_LINKS` | One object per DBA account (named by it): `{ account }`. Lookup is on the hot path of every DBA request, so it must not queue on a singleton like `SafetyDO`, and a per-DBA-account object makes "one DBA account → at most one cronly account" an atomic claim. Strongly consistent, unlike KV, which cronly does not use. |
+| Reverse link | `AccountDO` row `dba-link` | `{ dba_account, subject, issuer, linked_at }` for display and unlink. **It is the confirming half**: a request is accepted only when the index names the account *and* the account's row names the same DBA account. An interrupted link/unlink therefore authorizes nothing, and a stale claim can be taken over once its holder no longer confirms it (compare-and-swap `replace`). |
+| OIDC state | `AccountDO` rows `dba-oidc:<state>` | `{ state, nonce, verifier }`, 10-minute TTL, single use, at most 10 outstanding, pruned by `maintenance`. Never sent to the browser except the `state` value inside the authorization URL. |
+| Credential rows | `AccountDO` rows `dba-key:<cid>` | Written (only when changed) on each authenticated DBA request: DBA account, device id/name, credential expiry, monthly cap. Listed in the account's DBA section; deleted on unlink or relink; pruned after expiry. At most 1,000 per account. |
+| Budgets | `keyBudget` → `dbaKeyBudget` | The DBA credential id (`crd_…`, never a cronly key id) is the request's `key_id`, so every existing per-key path applies: reads are metered like API-key reads (21 µ$ + 2 µ$/job scanned), holds and charges carry `key_id`, and the grant's `limits.spend_usd_per_month` becomes a UTC-month key budget in µ$ (`Math.round(usd × 1e6)`; the tightest cap of any grant in the credential, as `createSiteAuth` reports it). Queued retries re-check the row, so unlink or expiry refuses them. |
+| MCP | `src/index.js` | `/mcp` replays each tool as an internal request. A DPoP proof is single-use and bound to `/mcp`, so the already-verified DBA identity is forwarded on the in-process `Request` object (a `WeakMap`; nothing an outside caller can set). |
+| Routes | `API_ROUTES` | `GET /api/dba/link` (status), `POST /api/dba/link/start`, `POST /api/dba/link/finish` (`{ code, state }`), `DELETE /api/dba/link`; all `SESSION`. Free like sign-in, rate-limited to 10 link operations a minute per account; `?dry_run=1` validates and quotes 0 µ$. |
+| UI | `frontend/js/app.js` | Account page "devicebasedauth.org" panel: link status, Link/Unlink, credentials seen, `dba enroll` and browser-SDK instructions. `/auth/dba/callback` serves `app.html`; it moves `code`/`state` out of the URL at once, then posts them to `finish` after sign-in. |
+
+### Why link/unlink are free
+
+They are session-only account settings, comparable to signing in (free). Each costs a few
+Durable Object requests and two outbound fetches to DBA, bounded by the per-account limit of 10
+per minute. If the owner prefers, bill them as a change (100 µ$) through `bill-change`.
+
+## Owner steps (ops)
+
+1. Deploy: the `DBA_LINKS` binding and migration `v3` (`new_sqlite_classes = ["DbaLinkDO"]`) are
+   in both wrangler configs. Add the new namespace's ID to `COST_SAFETY_DO_NAMESPACES` (CI
+   variable) so the cost monitor counts it.
+2. `DBA_ISSUER` is a plain `[vars]` entry (`https://devicebasedauth.org`; the code defaults to the
+   same). Point it elsewhere only for staging. For local work, run DBA's `dev:local` on another
+   port (both default to 8787) and set `DBA_ISSUER` in `wrangler.local.toml`; DBA's local issuer
+   must register `http://localhost:8787/auth/dba/callback` for client `cronly.org`.
+3. Confirm the registration at devicebasedauth.org: audience `cronly.org`, namespace `cronly`,
+   redirect URI `https://cronly.org/auth/dba/callback` (already in DBA's first-party list).
+4. Fund the cronly operator account at devicebasedauth.org. cronly verifies offline (free), so
+   this only matters if online authorization (`limits.uses`, `require.online`,
+   `require.step_up`) is enabled later; those grants deny today.
+5. Optional "Stage A" sign-in: register devicebasedauth.org as a custom OIDC provider in the
+   cronly Clerk application (discovery `https://devicebasedauth.org/.well-known/openid-configuration`,
+   client `cronly.org`, PKCE, scopes `openid profile`). That gives a "devicebasedauth.org" button
+   next to Google with no cronly code, but the result is an ordinary Clerk session (revoking the
+   device stops future sign-ins, not the session). Needs the Clerk plan to support custom
+   providers (DBA decision D7). It is independent of account linking.
+6. Live acceptance: link a real DBA account, `dba enroll --aud cronly.org --action cronly:read`,
+   call `GET /api/account` with `dba curl`, check the 21 µ$ read charge, revoke the device at DBA
+   and confirm 401 within the status-list TTL (≤ 5 minutes).
+
+## Follow-ups
+
+- Mark a `dba-key:` row revoked when the verifier reports `revoked`, so queued retries stop
+  immediately rather than at credential expiry or unlink.
+- Honor resource-scoped grants (`job:<name>`) per route.
+- Stage B (device-bound web sessions): let the web UI call the API with `dba.js` instead of
+  Clerk, then decide what `cronly:session` may do.
+- Online authorization for `limits.uses` / `require.*` grants (needs the funded operator
+  account and an API key from DBA).
